# Production build defaults. Vite loads this ONLY for `vite build`, never for
# `vite dev` — which is exactly what this file needs, because dev and prod reach
# the account API by two different routes:
#
#   dev    the base stays relative ('/v1') and vite.config.ts proxies it to
#          https://th-labs.uz. The proxy is not optional there: it also strips
#          `Secure` off the refresh cookie, which Safari otherwise refuses to
#          store over plain http://localhost, and sign-in silently never sticks.
#   build  there is no proxy. The base has to be the absolute URL, or every /v1
#          call resolves against the STUDIO's own origin, hits the SPA
#          catch-all, and sign-in dies with a 405 nobody sees.
#
# It exists because that second case had no committed default: `npm run build`
# produced a bundle that could not talk to anything, and only the Modal deploy
# happened to set the variable on the image. Modal still sets it — a real
# environment variable wins over a .env file in Vite — so this changes nothing
# there and fixes every other build.
#
# Everything here is PUBLIC: Vite inlines VITE_* into the JS bundle. Never put a
# key or a secret in this file.

# The NestJS account API: auth, users, payments, health, languages.
# Must include /v1. https://th-labs.uz/docs is the Swagger UI — a page for
# humans, not the API base. Pointing this at /docs makes every call land on
# /docs/payments/plans: a 404 with no CORS header.
VITE_ACCOUNT_API=https://th-labs.uz/v1

# Where signing out sends the browser. This app has no signed-out destination
# of its own — the landing site owns marketing and sign-in.
VITE_LANDING_URL=https://th-labs.uz

# VITE_DUB_API is deliberately NOT set.
#
# The dubbing pipeline (FastAPI: /api/jobs and /media playback) is served from
# the Studio's OWN origin in production, so the relative default is already
# right there. Hard-coding an absolute host here would break the deployed
# Studio, not fix it. Set it only for a bundle served from somewhere else.
